"# Messages, screens and agent settings\n\nSend messages and change one agent's overlays without modifying its definition.\n\n```sh\naos-agent say --target bob \"hello\"\naos-agent listen --target bob --timeout-s 60\naos-agent listen --target bob --last 3 --follow\naos-agent tools --target bob add --name read ../lib/base/read\naos-agent llm --target bob set model=smart-high\n```\n\nsay prints a letter name. Words are joined with spaces. No words, or `-`, reads stdin; terminal stdin with no words is an error. Paused/sleeping agents still queue messages. listen waits for the latest question by default and prints only answer text. Without a timeout it can wait forever. `--last N` prints question/answer blocks. `--follow` (`-f`) follows new exchanges, with or without `--last`; alone it follows only new answers. Do not combine `--timeout-s` with `--last` or `--follow`. Ctrl-C in follow exits 0; while waiting it exits 130.\n\nchat sends one line at a time and waits for each answer. Ctrl-C leaves. open/close/watch require a declared screen interface. `open --detach` opens without attaching. Without a screen, use listen.\n\n`aos-agent tools --target bob ls|get NAME|add [--name N] [--replace] [--envs K=V]... [--env-file FILE] PATH|rm NAME|reset` manages the toolbox (choose one action). No action means ls. Changes are saved in this agent's own `tools.json` in its stack, which holds only the differences from the definition's (`\"$override\"`; a removed tool is `null`); the definition is not changed. add/rm can print the same result lines as aos-tools. reset deletes the overlay; see `aos/cli/safety.json`.\n\n`aos-agent llm --target bob ls|set KEY=VALUE...|rm KEY...|reset` manages changed brain keys. No action means ls; `ls -l` also shows which keys were changed for this agent. `model` picks the brain pack; `temperature`, `top_p`, `max_tokens`, `seed`, `presence_penalty`, `frequency_penalty`, `stop` tune it. rm drops single changed keys. Only changed keys are stored; other keys follow the definition. Values are strings. reset removes changes.\n\n`aos-agent system --target bob get [-l | --json]|set [FILE | -]|reset [-y]` shows or replaces this agent's system text (no action means get). set reads FILE, or stdin when FILE is `-` or not given; the text goes whole into `<stack>/system.txt` and replaces the definition's (an agent-fsm sends it as its system message; an eca appends it to the CLI's own). The definition must declare `{\"aos-agent\": {\"system\": \"system.txt\"}}`, otherwise `SystemMissing`. reset deletes the copy. `llm set system=...` is not the way: it warns and points here.\n\nBrain service calls are separate: `aos-llm ls` lists endpoint packs; pipe a request letter into `aos-llm ask`. It needs `AOS_LLM_KERNEL`.\n"
